From: SM-G955N Ubuntu port contributors
Subject: [PROPOSAL] trace: exynos-ss: retain bounded work history in panic log

Diagnostic candidate only; excluded from the automatic patches/*.patch chain.
No claim of fixing the worker/RCU corruption observed in kernel #22.

The existing ESS work ring records callback start/end even in minimized mode,
but is cleared on the next boot. Copy its last 32 records per CPU to the normal
panic log before the large task dump, so the existing last_kmsg can preserve a
bounded recent history without enabling full function tracing or changing the
normal workqueue hot path. Dump only once and only after verifying the ring's
initialized mapping/size. Do not re-enable logging after show_regs stopped it.

Records are best-effort value snapshots: another CPU might have been writing an
entry when stopped. Recorded worker/task/function pointers must never be
followed in the panic dumper; print raw pointer values for offline symbolization.
Bound task names explicitly. No allocation, new lock, control-register write,
service operation, or new memory reservation is introduced. The existing atomic
once guard and printk path are used only on panic. Current and other CPUs are
all included because corruption may originate on a different CPU.

Validation must check bounded/wrapped indexing, empty/uninitialized rings,
nonterminated names, invalid recorded pointers and one-time output. A successful
build/model test does not establish live panic persistence or the corruption's
root cause. The addresses/logs must remain private with the device evidence.

diff --git a/drivers/trace/exynos-ss.c b/drivers/trace/exynos-ss.c
--- a/drivers/trace/exynos-ss.c
+++ b/drivers/trace/exynos-ss.c
@@ -2166,6 +2166,45 @@
 	return 0;
 }
 
+/* Diagnostic only: bounded value snapshots, never follow recorded pointers.
+ * The work ring already exists in minimized mode and show_regs may have
+ * disabled new writes before this panic notifier runs.
+ */
+static void exynos_ss_dump_work_tail(void)
+{
+	static atomic64_t dumped = ATOMIC64_INIT(0);
+	struct exynos_ss_item *item;
+	unsigned int cpu, n, slot;
+	unsigned int mask = ARRAY_SIZE(ess_log->work[0]) - 1;
+	int head;
+	struct work_log record;
+
+	if (!ess_base.enabled || !ess_log)
+		return;
+	item = &ess_items[ess_desc.kevents_num];
+	if (!item->entry.enabled_init || !item->entry.vaddr ||
+			item->entry.size < ESS_HEADER_TOTAL_SZ + sizeof(*ess_log) ||
+			(unsigned long)ess_log != item->entry.vaddr + ESS_HEADER_TOTAL_SZ)
+		return;
+	if (atomic64_cmpxchg(&dumped, 0, 1))
+		return;
+
+	pr_emerg("ESS_WORK_TAIL v1 last=32_per_cpu best_effort raw_pointers_only\n");
+	for (cpu = 0; cpu < ARRAY_SIZE(ess_log->work); cpu++) {
+		head = atomic_read(&ess_idx.work_log_idx[cpu]);
+		for (n = 32; n > 0; n--) {
+			slot = ((unsigned int)head - (n - 1)) & mask;
+			memcpy(&record, &ess_log->work[cpu][slot], sizeof(record));
+			if (!record.time)
+				continue;
+			record.task_comm[TASK_COMM_LEN - 1] = '\0';
+			pr_emerg("ESS_WORK cpu=%u slot=%u time_ns=%llu worker=%p fn=%p sp=%lx en=%d comm=%.*s\n",
+				cpu, slot, record.time, record.worker, record.fn,
+				record.sp, record.en, TASK_COMM_LEN - 1, record.task_comm);
+		}
+	}
+}
+
 static int exynos_ss_panic_handler(struct notifier_block *nb,
 				   unsigned long l, void *buf)
 {
@@ -2183,6 +2222,7 @@
 #else
 	pr_emerg("exynos-snapshot: panic - normal[%s]\n", __func__);
 #endif
+	exynos_ss_dump_work_tail();
 	exynos_ss_dump_task_info();
 	flush_cache_all();
 #ifdef CONFIG_SEC_DEBUG
